<h1>EcoBreathe: Predictive Analysis of Urban Air Quality & Municipal Public Health Vulnerability</h1>
<p style="font-size: 13pt; color: #3b82f6; font-style: italic;">A Data-Driven Analytical Framework for Smarter Environmental Governance and Healthcare Preparedness</p>

<p><strong>United Nations Sustainable Development Goal Alignment:</strong> Good Health and Well-being (SDG 3) & Sustainable Cities and Communities (SDG 11) (Goal 3 & Goal 11)<br>
<strong>Kaggle Dataset Source:</strong> https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/hasibalmuzzamil/air-quality-and-health-impact-dataset</p>

<h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>
<p>Rapid urbanization and unchecked industrial emissions have escalated urban ambient air pollution into an urgent global public health emergency. This project delivers an end-to-end data analysis pipeline evaluating five criteria pollutants (PM2.5, PM10, NO2, SO2, CO) and meteorological variables across metropolitan micro-zones. Through rigorous exploratory data analysis, correlation modelling, and machine learning classification (Random Forest with 93.8% test accuracy), we uncover that fine particulate matter (PM2.5) and nitrogen dioxide (NO2) account for 68% of the predictive variance in severe respiratory emergency admissions. The project provides an interactive simulation tool and policy recommendations enabling municipal agencies to forecast high-risk episodes 48 hours in advance.</p>

<h2>2. Problem Statement & Scope</h2>
<p>Metropolitan health authorities often operate reactively rather than proactively during severe smog episodes. Because air pollution spikes trigger sudden influxes of acute asthma, COPD exacerbations, and pediatric respiratory distress, municipal hospitals frequently face critical bed shortages and oxygen demand surges. The core analytical problem is establishing the quantitative relationship between multi-pollutant concentrations, ambient weather conditions, and subsequent respiratory emergency caseloads to build an early warning forecasting model.</p>
<h3>Core Project Objectives:</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Collect, clean, and standardize multi-sensor urban air quality and clinical admission datasets.</li><li>Conduct comprehensive exploratory data analysis (EDA) to evaluate pollutant distribution and temporal variance.</li><li>Quantify statistical correlations between individual atmospheric contaminants and respiratory hospital admissions.</li><li>Train and compare machine learning classifiers (Logistic Regression, Random Forest, XGBoost) to predict Air Quality Health Risk tiers with >90% precision.</li><li>Develop actionable municipal mitigation protocols and clinical resource scheduling policies based on empirical data.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Scope & Limitations:</strong> The study incorporates 1,250 temporal observations across 6 distinct municipal zones spanning residential, industrial, high-traffic commercial, and greenbelt areas over a 12-month monitoring cycle. The modeling scope focuses on 5 core atmospheric pollutants and 2 meteorological indices.</p>

<h2>3. Dataset Description & Sourcing</h2>
<p><strong>Source:</strong> Kaggle Open Air Quality & Health Impact Dataset / Central Pollution Control Board Repository<br>
<strong>Volume:</strong> 1250 records across 14 features.<br>
<strong>Target Variable:</strong> Health_Risk_Level (Categorical) / Respiratory_Admissions (Continuous)</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Feature Name</th>
      <th>Data Type</th>
